@prefix review: <http://example.org/agora/review#> .
@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/agora/sensing#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix ssn:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix ssn-system: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/systems/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .

<http://example.org/agora/sensing> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Agora sensing" ;
    rdfs:comment """Sensing: what an agent can KNOW and DECIDE about a subject's state. Split by WHO HOLDS THE CLOCK, and deliberately independent of how a device is reached — a scheduled board on a bus and one on a GPIO pin present the same ability and the same choices, so the capability is the same and only the BINDING differs (see mqtt.ttl). The line: a capability distinguishes what an agent must decide; a binding distinguishes how a device is spoken to.""" .

#################  The capabilities  #################
#  Three, and the axis is WHO HOLDS THE CLOCK — because that is what changes what the agent
#  must believe, which is the test for whether something deserves to be a capability at all.
#  Protocol does NOT change it: an agent's attention policy has no business depending on a
#  wire format.
#
#      sensing:Polling      the AGENT's clock   — it asks, each time
#      sensing:Subscribing  a SHARED clock      — the agent states an interval, the device keeps it
#      sensing:Listening    the DEVICE's clock  — it announces, and takes no orders
#
#  They are strictly decreasing in agent control, and each one asks the agent for strictly
#  less. Only the middle one is implemented today; see the note on sensing:Polling.

sensing:SensingCapability a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Sensing capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "The family. Rules true of sensing however it is done target this class, so nothing is restated per transport, and one capability may ask for 'whoever perceives' without knowing which of the three answered." .

sensing:Polling a sensing:SensingCapability ; rdfs:label "Polling" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent holds the clock and ASKS for each reading: one request, one response. The simplest possible exchange, and the one with the most agent control — but it requires a device that is reachable at any moment, so it cannot be had from a board that deep-sleeps between readings. RESERVED: declared so the vocabulary is honest about the three, but no derivation rule grants it and no module implements it, so no agent can hold it yet." .

sensing:Subscribing a sensing:SensingCapability ; rdfs:label "Subscribing" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent states an INTERVAL and the device keeps to it: a standing request rather than a repeated one. The agent still owns the policy — how often to look is entirely its choice — but it delegates the timekeeping, which is what lets the device sleep between readings and is why a battery board can be perceived at all. What it gives up is asking at a moment of its choosing." .

sensing:Listening a sensing:SensingCapability ; rdfs:label "Listening" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent RECEIVES readings on the device's own schedule and states no interval at all. It keeps its freshness rule — which becomes a liveness detector rather than a control — but is never asked for a cadence it could not apply." .

#################  Sensors  #################

#  `sensing:Sensor` was here, and it was `sosa:Sensor` wearing our name.
#
#  It was an intersection, not a synonym — `ag:Device ∧ sosa:Sensor` — and that is why the earlier
#  audits kept it. But a named class is not the only way to state an intersection: an instance
#  can carry both types, and the two constraints can sit side by side in the one shape that
#  enforced the conjunction. What the name bought was saying it once; what it cost was a reader
#  having to learn that our word for a sensor is not the standard one.
#
#  `ag:Device` STAYS, and why is the principle this whole audit turns on.
#
#  SOSA's axis is FUNCTIONAL: `sosa:Platform` asks *where is it mounted* — its own examples are
#  a post, a buoy, a ship, a cell-phone, a human — and `ssn:System` asks *what implements the
#  procedure*. Neither is about what a thing is made of, and the spec is explicit that all its
#  major classes may be virtual, a correction it made deliberately.
#
#  `ag:Device`'s axis is SUBSTRATE: a physical edge node with legs, interchangeable with a
#  stand-in. That is a different question, so a term answering it necessarily cuts ACROSS their
#  classes rather than duplicating one — `mc:Microcontroller` and `mc:Peripheral` are Platforms,
#  sensors and actuators are Systems, and `mc:hasPin`, `mc:logicVolts` and `ag:simulatedBy` all
#  take `ag:Device` as their domain. Boards have pins.
#
#  So the test for keeping one of ours is not whether a standard has a similar word. It is
#  whether the standard is answering the same QUESTION. Here it is not, which is why
#  `sensing:Sensor` goes and `ag:Device` stays.
#
#  See knowledge/decisions/one-word-for-one-relation.md.


# BORROWED, AND RESTATED — the one axiom this project holds about somebody else's class.
#
# `ssn-system:hasSystemCapability` hangs off an `ssn:System`, and what makes it reach a sensor is
# `sosa:Sensor rdfs:subClassOf ssn:System`. That is asserted in <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> —
# neither in SOSA nor in the systems module — and nothing here loads it. Measured before this
# line existed: three things typed `sosa:Sensor` in `world/sensing`, zero typed `ssn:System`.
#
# So the general shape, which is worth more than the line: borrowing an IRI brings its
# DEFINITION and none of the axioms other documents state ABOUT it. For each borrowed term, ask
# whether what makes it apply arrives with the IRI or has to be restated. `mc:carries
# rdfs:subPropertyOf sosa:hosts` is the same move from the other direction.
#
# HERE rather than in `packages/core/agora/`, though the subject is a class this package does not
# own: the axiom is only load-bearing because sensors have capabilities, and sensing is where
# a sensor is. A package restates what it needs to be true; the kernel would be asserting it on
# behalf of a package that may not be installed.
sosa:Sensor rdfs:subClassOf ssn:System .

sensing:polls a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:label "polls" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent may read this sensor. This IS the access grant — expressed as topology rather than as a separate permission system, so isolation is visible in the wiring." .

sensing:monitors a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:label "monitors" ;
    rdfs:comment "The subject this sensor reads. Pairs with sosa:observes, which says WHICH property." .

#################  How a device is driven — a fact about the hardware  #################
# The agent's capability is derived from this, so ability can never drift from the device.
# One sense mode per capability, and the pairing is the whole derivation: what an agent can
# decide about looking follows from what its hardware can be told.

# A sense mode IS a `sosa:Procedure` — SOSA's definition is *a workflow, protocol, plan,
# algorithm, or computational method specifying how to make an Observation*, and who holds the
# clock is exactly that. So the three below are typed as Procedures as well, which makes them
# legible to anything speaking SSN.
#
# The RELATION stays ours, and that is the part worth explaining. `ssn:implements` is what SSN
# uses, and it is wider: a System may implement several Procedures. `sensing:senseMode`
# carries `sh:maxCount 1`, because a sensor has exactly one answer to *who holds the clock* and
# the derivation is guarded on it — `sensing:Subscribing` from ScheduledProcedure, `Listening`
# from PushProcedure. Swapping the predicate would either lose that count or, keeping it,
# forbid a sensor ever implementing a second procedure of any kind. Neither is worth the tidiness.
#
# So: the VALUES defer to the standard, the RELATION does not, and the reason is a cardinality
# ours checks and theirs cannot.
#
# **The clock procedure is already here — do not add a second one.** Once parts began declaring
# `ssn:implements` it looked as though something was missing: a board keeps time, and nothing
# said so. It does say so. These three ARE the clock, differing only in who sets it — and the
# statement sits on the DEVICE that keeps it (#96): the thing that connects and sleeps, never
# the sensing element, which no more holds a clock than a thermometer holds an alarm. A
# peripheral's mode is its board's, found through the stream they share, so two sensors on one
# board CANNOT disagree about who holds its clock — the disagreement is unrepresentable rather
# than refused. See knowledge/decisions/a-procedure-belongs-to-whatever-performs-it.md and
# knowledge/decisions/who-holds-the-clock.md.

#################  Deployment — hosting is what a deployment produces  #################
#
#  SSN's own axiom, restated because we borrow IRIs and never import ontologies — the same move
#  `sosa:Sensor rdfs:subClassOf ssn:System` needed (#94). In SSN, `sosa:hosts` carries this
#  property chain: a platform in a deployment that deploys a system HOSTS that system. We used
#  to assert the conclusion with the premise nowhere in the graph (#99); the wiring now states
#  the deployment — this board, these systems, from this date, for this purpose — and the
#  closure entails the hosting from it. The society's copy in world.ttl stays ASSERTED,
#  deliberately: an agent is never handed the wiring, so the society restates what it may know,
#  and test_layout holds the two to agreeing exactly as before.

sosa:hosts owl:propertyChainAxiom ( ssn:inDeployment ssn:deployedSystem ) .

sensing:SenseMode a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Sense mode" ;
    rdfs:comment "The class exists for the count: the speaking-device shape asks for exactly one, and `sosa:Procedure` is too wide to say that with. Its members are Procedures too." .

sensing:PolledProcedure a sensing:SenseMode , sosa:Procedure ; rdfs:label "Polled procedure" ;
    rdfs:comment "Always reachable, and answers each request: ask, and it reads and replies. Gives sensing:Polling. A board that deep-sleeps is NOT this, however it is asked — it cannot hear the request. RESERVED alongside sensing:Polling: no rule maps it yet." .

sensing:ScheduledProcedure a sensing:SenseMode , sosa:Procedure ; rdfs:label "Scheduled procedure" ;
    rdfs:comment "Takes an interval and keeps to it, sleeping in between: it accepts instruction about WHEN, then clocks itself. Gives sensing:Subscribing. This is what a deep-sleeping battery board is, and the reason the middle capability exists at all." .

sensing:PushProcedure a sensing:SenseMode , sosa:Procedure ; rdfs:label "Push procedure" ;
    rdfs:comment "Self-clocked: it announces on its own cadence and takes no commands. Gives sensing:Listening. The edge holds the policy, and no agent can take it back." .

#  The THIRD clock-holder, finally earning its place (#151): not the agent's (Scheduled), not
#  the device's (Push) — the WORLD's. A device implementing this watches thresholds while it
#  deep-sleeps (an ESP32's ULP compares the reading every second for microamps) and wakes to
#  announce the moment the value CROSSES one. The device is merely the world's messenger: the
#  signal changed, so the signal says so.
#
#  Deliberately NOT a fourth SenseMode. The mode is who holds the HEARTBEAT, one answer per
#  device (#96) — a crossing-watcher still keeps the interval its agent commands, and the
#  crossing wake is an ADDITIONAL procedure the same device implements. Which is exactly the
#  case the senseMode comment above always predicted: ssn:implements is wider than our
#  relation BECAUSE a system may implement several procedures, and here is the second one,
#  arriving through the standard word rather than by widening ours.
#
#  What it PROMISES is the part the agent may lean on: silence from a crossing-armed board
#  means "nothing crossed" — information — where silence from a plain scheduled board means
#  only "not due yet". The thresholds themselves are the agent's to command, retained beside
#  the cadence, and the natural ones are its region's edges: the board then literally watches
#  the agent's desire while both of them sleep.
sensing:CrossingProcedure a sosa:Procedure ; rdfs:label "Announce on crossing" ;
    rdfs:comment "Watch commanded thresholds while asleep; wake and publish when the value crosses one. Stated on the connecting DEVICE with ssn:implements, beside (never instead of) its sense mode. A shape requires the device to be Scheduled with a command channel, because thresholds arrive the way the cadence does — a push board that watched thresholds would have no way to be told which." .

sensing:senseMode a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain <http://example.org/agora#Device> ; rdfs:range sensing:SenseMode ;
    rdfs:label "sense mode" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whose fact this is decided #96: the DEVICE's — the board that sleeps, wakes and keeps the interval — never the sensing element's. Three sensors on one board used to state it three times with no shape spanning them, so one could claim Scheduled while its neighbour claimed Push: a physical impossibility that would have derived both Subscribing and Listening for one agent from one firmware. Stated once on the connecting device now, and a bearer shape (transport/mqtt) refuses it anywhere else." .

sensing:samples a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ; rdfs:range <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/Sample> ;
    rdfs:label "samples" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which PATCH this probe actually sits in (#98) — a sosa:Sample of the subject it monitors. SOSA's own words: a Sample is representative of a FeatureOfInterest that is not fully accessible, and a pot's soil is exactly that; the W3C's deployment example does the same with a room, measuring a wall and treating it as the room's witness. Optional, and the option is the point: someone has to JUDGE that two probes sit in two patches rather than one, and no shape can make that judgement — absent a sample, the subject is the feature and everything reads as it always did, exactly as mqtt:readingPointer defaults to /value. Where two probes do state two patches, each owns its observation node and the pot answers with the newest across them — kept as testimony per patch, never aggregated, which stays the seam one-agent-many-sensors.md left open. NOT sosa:Sampler/Sampling territory: nothing here extracts a specimen; a patch is soil somebody decided to treat as representative, not something a machine produced." .

#################  Attention — the agent's private policy  #################

# Required only of an agent that can actually act on them: driving a device is what makes a
# cadence meaningful, so the transport's rules decide whether these are demanded.
sensing:fastSleepS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "fast sleep seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "Cadence when the agent judges itself in trouble — how closely it watches when worried." .
sensing:slowSleepS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "slow sleep seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "Cadence when comfortable. Attention costs energy, so looking less is a real choice." .

# Required of EVERY perceiving agent, however it perceives: deciding what is too old to act
# on is about belief, not control, so it survives losing the ability to drive the device.
sensing:readingGraceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "reading grace" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long past the interval it ASKED for a subscribing agent still trusts a reading — a board's wake time and a late wifi association. Relative on purpose: an agent that chose to look every 600s cannot coherently refuse a 120s-old number, and an absolute limit made every comfortable plant look like a failed sensor." .

sensing:maxReadingAgeS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "max reading age seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How stale a reading may be and still back a decision. Without it, 'I choose when to look' becomes 'I choose which number to believe'." .

#################  The constitutional bounds — stated, not compiled in  #################

sensing:minSleepS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Capability ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "minimum sleep seconds" .
sensing:maxSleepS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Capability ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "maximum sleep seconds" .

sensing:relaxFactor a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Capability ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "relax factor" ;
    rdfs:comment "Fast attack, slow release (#139). Tightening is INSTANTANEOUS — any voice in the urgency choir earns its cadence at once, because hesitating in that direction costs a plant — but relaxing is bounded per commanded step: the next sleep may exceed the last by at most this factor, so one comfortable reading cannot cliff a burst-tight cadence straight to the slow end. The release then runs geometrically (30, 45, 68, 101...), a few minutes of dense sampling during which the trend establishes and the #133 bound takes over — confidence is EARNED per reading, not assumed from one. On the family like the bounds, because how fast a society lets its members stop looking is the society's figure, not a compile-time constant." .

sensing:SensingCapability sensing:minSleepS 10 ; sensing:maxSleepS 900 ;
    sensing:relaxFactor 1.5 .

#################  What the DEVICE can honour, which is a different question  #################
#
# The two figures above are what the society ALLOWS. They say nothing about what a particular
# board can do, and until now nothing could: `ranges()` has documented a third source —
# "hardware limits when any exist" — since it was written, and none existed. An agent could
# therefore ask for a ten-second cadence a board would never keep, and `stale_after_s` would
# compute freshness from the interval it ASKED for, so a healthy board read as a quiet one.
#
# Borrowed rather than invented, because SSN already says exactly this. `ssn-system:Frequency`:
#
#     "The smallest possible time between one Observation, Actuation, or Sampling and the next,
#      under the defined Conditions."
#
# A TIME, despite the name — the smallest gap, which is a floor and maps onto review:notBelow
# with nothing to convert. And `under the defined Conditions` is in the definition itself, which
# is why a capability is qualified by an `ssn-system:Condition` rather than being one number
# with a caveat: "ten seconds on mains" and "fifteen minutes on battery" are two capabilities of
# one device, and #78's argument is precisely that the right wake depends on the power budget.
#
# What this package adds is the VALUE, because SSN deliberately leaves values to other
# vocabularies. The class carries the meaning; this carries the number.

#  `sensing:seconds` was here, and schema.org already had the idiom.
#
#  SSN leaves values to other vocabularies, and the W3C's own worked example of this very part
#  — `ssn/integrated/examples/dht22.ttl`, the DHT11's sibling, written by the people who wrote
#  the vocabulary — says it like this:
#
#      <DHT22/4578#TemperatureSensorFrequency> a ssn:Property , ssn-system:Frequency ,
#          schema:PropertyValue ; schema:value 2 ; schema:unitCode qudt-unit-1-1:Second .
#
#  A number and its unit, rather than a property named for the unit. Their example predates
#  current QUDT, so the idiom is theirs and the IRI is ours: `unit:SEC`, the prefix #77 already
#  declared. That is the half-adopted idiom completed — #77 put unit IRIs on sensors and then
#  wrote the figure beside them in a term of our own.
#
#  Their datasheet figure is 2 seconds, which is what the KY-015 declares here. Independent
#  corroboration of a number nobody here measured.
#
#  The `rdfs:range xsd:integer` this term carried was never enforced — `rdfs:range` is not in
#  `agent/inference.py`'s closure, the same way `mc:carries`'s domain was not. It is a shape
#  now (`sensing:StatedFrequencyShape`), so the swap gains a check rather than losing one.

# BORROWED, AND BRIDGED — the legacy spelling of somebody else's namespace.
#
# schema.org migrated to `https:` and calls that its preferred form, while `http:` "will remain
# widely understood for the foreseeable future and there should be no urgency about migrating
# existing data". Both are in the wild, and RDF does not care what a registry prefers: they are
# two IRIs. `<http://schema.org/value>` does not satisfy a shape written against
# `<https://schema.org/value>`, and nothing says so — the property is simply absent.
#
# The idiom above was copied FROM the W3C's DHT22 example, and that example is written in the
# legacy form. So a description straight from the people who wrote the vocabulary states its
# frequency in a property our shape cannot see. Measured, before this bridge existed: all three
# violations that example raised about its frequency were this, and not the QUDT version they
# look like — two `minCount` misses reported as "must be in seconds" and "must carry one whole
# number", for a node that states both, correctly, in the other scheme.
#
# One direction only, and deliberately. Legacy ENTAILS canonical, so a vendor's file satisfies
# our shapes; nothing here ever emits the legacy form, and a bridge the other way would invite
# it. `agent/inference.py` rule 4 carries this into the world's entailed graph — the rule
# written for exactly this shape of fact, whose own comment records that it had nothing left to
# exercise after `mc:carries` went.
<http://schema.org/value>    rdfs:subPropertyOf schema:value .
<http://schema.org/unitCode> rdfs:subPropertyOf schema:unitCode .

#################  Re-picking the comfortable cadence  #################
# ONE triple makes a belief revisable, and it names the two figures already stated above. The
# reviewer finds it by asking the merged T-Box — nothing lists it and no Python knows it, which
# is what keeps this a fact about the TERM rather than a fact about anyone holding it.
#
# Only the comfortable end. `cadence_for` interpolates from it toward sensing:fastSleepS by urgency,
# so a thirsty agent still watches at its fastest whatever a review concluded: attention follows
# need first, and follows whether looking is telling it anything only inside the room need
# leaves. That composition is the whole reason it is safe to let an agent slow itself down.

sensing:slowSleepS a review:RevisableBelief ;
    review:revisableToward sensing:minSleepS , sensing:maxSleepS .

# How still counts as still, and how much evidence is enough to say so. Constitutional rather
# than private for the same reason the sleep bounds are: left to each agent, these are the two
# numbers a lazy one tunes until it is entitled to stop looking.

sensing:steadyFraction a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Capability ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "steady fraction" ;
    rdfs:comment "How far a reading may range, as a fraction of its own mean, and still count as unchanging. Relative rather than absolute because one agent's property is a fraction and another's is degrees, and a threshold in either unit is nonsense in the other." .

sensing:reviewWindow a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Capability ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "review window" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many readings must have accumulated before an agent may draw a conclusion about them. Guards the failure this mechanism could otherwise cause: two identical readings are a coincidence, and an agent that relaxed its attention on the strength of them would be reasoning from noise." .

sensing:SensingCapability sensing:steadyFraction 0.02 ; sensing:reviewWindow 20 .
